SADIE II HRTF data

Headphone surround uses head-related impulse responses from the SADIE II database,
Copyright 2018, University of York (AudioLab, Department of Electronic Engineering),
licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 (https://zenodo.org/records/10886409,
subject D1, Neumann KU100, 48 kHz). Armstrong, Thresh, Murphy and Kearney, "A Perceptual
Evaluation of Individual and Non-Individual HRTFs: A Case Study of the SADIE II
Database", Applied Sciences 8(11):2029, 2018. Changes made by Rubylight: nine horizontal
directions selected, diffuse-field equalized, converted to minimum phase with their
measured arrival times, left and right symmetrized, common onset trimmed and truncated
to 256 taps (rust/tools/hrir/generate.py). The license text is in rust/THIRD_PARTY.md
and rust/core/src/audio_dsp/LICENSE-SADIE-II.txt.

Moonlight V+

Several Nightjar decoder and input behaviours follow Moonlight V+
(https://github.com/qiin2333/moonlight-vplus) by qiin2333 and contributors, GPL-3.0:
the declared-only Snapdragon decoder keys and the shorter MediaTek queues on the
first configure try (VendorLowLatencyKeys.java), leaving out the HEVC low-latency
flags on Amlogic decoders (MediaCodecHelper.java), growing the decoder input buffer
instead of ending the stream (MediaCodecDecoderRenderer.java), and the controller
gyroscope liveness check (GyroLivenessTracker.java, ControllerHandler.java), all
under client/app/src/main/java/com/limelight.

Clipboard sync (control message 0x5508) uses Moonlight V+'s frame and behaviour
(moonlight-common-c PR #5 in the V+ fork, ClipboardSyncManager.kt, MoonBridge.java;
GPL-3.0), so a V+ client syncs with the host unchanged: the frame and its echo tokens
in client/protocol/core/src/clipboard_sync.rs, the rules in
client/protocol/client/src/control/clipboard_sync.rs, the host's side in
rust/host/src/stream/clipboard_sync.rs, and Nightjar's in
client/app/src/main/java/com/limelight/utils/ClipboardSync.java and
client/protocol/desktop/src/stream/clipboard_sync.rs.

Nightjar desktop's AI enhancement on Windows follows Moonlight V+ PC
(https://github.com/qiin2333/moonlight-qt, GPL-3.0, d3d11va.cpp and amfsession.cpp):
the per-vendor probe, the NVIDIA and Intel video processor extensions, RTX Video HDR,
the AMF HQ Scaler session and its sharpness, in
client/protocol/desktop/src/stream/enhance.rs and amf_scaler.rs.
Its drawing of D3D11VA pictures from the decoder's own textures, with the decoder
textures made shader-readable on Intel GPUs as V+ does, follows the same d3d11va.cpp;
the HDR tone map following HDR10+ metadata or the measured frame peak follows V+'s
plvk.cpp and libplacebo's peak detection defaults (client/protocol/desktop/src/stream/direct.rs).
Its Vulkan video decoding on Linux is ported from moonlight-qt's plvk.cpp
(https://github.com/moonlight-stream/moonlight-qt, GPL-3.0, Cameron Gutman and
contributors) as Moonlight V+ ships it: FFmpeg's Vulkan device context filled from the
renderer's own Vulkan device, its queue locks and the frame locks around the presenter's
copy (client/protocol/desktop/src/vulkan_video.rs).
Its drawing straight to the display on Linux follows moonlight-qt v6.2.0's DRM renderer
(app/streaming/video/ffmpeg-renderers/drm.cpp, same project and licence): sharing SDL's
KMSDRM descriptor, choosing the video plane and its zpos under the overlay, and the
connector and plane properties for HDR metadata, colorimetry, max bpc, YCbCr encoding and
range (client/protocol/desktop/src/kms/ and stream/kms_scanout.rs).
Nightjar desktop's high-performance GPU power while streaming on Linux, which holds an AMD
GPU that drives a connected display at its peak stable power state (client/protocol/desktop/
src/stream/gpu_hold.rs), is ported from Nonary's moonlight-qt fork
(https://github.com/Nonary/moonlight-qt, GPL-3.0): app/streaming/gpuperformancehold.{h,cpp}
at commit 19758e5e6, with the same GPU selection, requests and release.
The FFmpeg that Nightjar desktop ships on Windows carries Moonlight's FFmpeg patch for
D3D11VA, D3D12VA and DXVA2 decoding of HEVC Range Extension profiles (HEVC 4:4:4), by
Cameron Gutman, from https://github.com/moonlight-stream/moonlight-qt-deps
(patches/ffmpeg_dxva_hevc_rext.patch), adapted to FFmpeg 9.0
(client/protocol/desktop/ffmpeg-patches/windows/). It is part of FFmpeg (LGPL-2.1 or
later) and ships with FFmpeg's licence and source note.

